{"content":[{"id":"5f02d0e0","settings":{"content_width":"boxed","boxed_width":{"size":900,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"padding":{"top":0,"right":0,"bottom":0,"left":0,"unit":"px","isLinked":true}},"elements":[{"id":"2849d604","settings":{"design_skin":"contrast","uf_eyebrow":"AFTER HOURS \/ CITY STORIES","title":"The city, at another pace.","title_tag":"h3","content_source":"editor","editor_content":"<p>When the last commuters leave the station, another rhythm begins. Bakers prepare the morning dough. A cleaner opens the doors of an empty theatre. A florist checks the first delivery.<\/p><p>This series follows the people whose working day lives between the usual hours. Their stories reveal a city that is familiar in outline and completely different in detail.<\/p><p>We walk, listen and return. The photographs come slowly, shaped by trust and conversation. Together they form a portrait of work that often goes unseen, and the people who keep a place moving.<\/p>","collapsed_height":{"size":170,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"collapsed_height_mobile":{"size":190,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"initial_state":"folded","unfold_duration":420,"expand_label":"Continue reading","collapse_label":"Show less","trigger":"click","show_button":"yes","show_overlay":"yes","hide_trigger_if_fits":"yes","uf_accent":"#c7ef8d","uf_focus":"#c7ef8d","box_padding":{"top":42,"right":44,"bottom":42,"left":44,"unit":"px","isLinked":false},"box_padding_mobile":{"top":28,"right":24,"bottom":28,"left":24,"unit":"px","isLinked":false},"box_gap":{"size":22,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"title_typography_typography":"custom","title_typography_font_family":"Georgia","title_typography_font_size":{"size":42,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"title_typography_font_size_mobile":{"size":29,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"title_typography_font_weight":"500","title_typography_line_height":{"size":1.12,"unit":"em","sizes":[]},"content_typography_typography":"custom","content_typography_font_family":"Arial","content_typography_font_size":{"size":16